00:00:28.464 --> 00:00:31.460
KAZ SATO: In this session,
we try to cover these topics.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.522
First, I directly
discuss about how

00:00:33.522 --> 00:00:35.230
you can build a scalable
machine learning

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.700
production system with
Google Cloud's AI platform.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.550
Then I will discuss
about the second problem

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.940
that is called siloed ML
and how you can solve that

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.150
by using ML service mesh.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.780
In the latter part
of this session,

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.320
Rakesh will discuss
about API management

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.900
and how it can help
building ML service mesh.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.240
So let's start
discussing about how

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.340
you can build a scalable
machine learning system

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.090
with AI platform.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.720
Now many data
scientists or companies

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.680
are trying to bring the PoC
system into a production

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.080
machine learning system.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.250
But those PoC or prototypes can
be all covered by a single data

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.920
scientist, but for
production system,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.110
you have to split
the responsibility

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.410
into the multiple roles.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.540
For example, data
engineer can be

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.650
responsible for ingesting
the data from data warehouse

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:36.000
and apply some
pre-processing against that,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.620
whereas data scientists
and ML engineers

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.820
can be responsible for
training the module,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.310
design the machine learning
model, get the higher accuracy,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.840
and to push the training
model into the system.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.600
And then ops engineer
or ML engineer

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.970
can be responsible for designing
the scalable and continuous

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.410
training and prediction system.

00:02:02.098 --> 00:02:06.140
We'll think about a scenario
where the data scientist

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.720
is visiting a cubicle
of the ops teams

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.970
to ask to bring the PoC
systems into production system.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:18.420
What kind of the problems
the ops team have to solve?

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.220
There are many different gaps
between PoC and production.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.410
For example, many
PoC notebooks can

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.140
be locally dependent or
dependent on a single person.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.410
But with production
system, you have

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:34.070
to make everything
portable and shareable.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.500
And you cannot rely on a single
notebook instance anymore.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.500
You have to think
about build a cluster

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.280
of those multiple instances
with the scaling out or scaling

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.950
in functionalities
or load balancing

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.100
of the incoming
prediction requests.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.140
And everything should
not be dependent

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.910
on the manual process.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.190
Everything should
be reproducible

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.110
for the end-to-end machine
learning life cycle.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.920
And lastly, you have to
think about monitoring

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.800
so that ops team can be
responsible for the continuous

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:09.670
monitoring and routing for
the continuous operation.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.380
So though that the reason
why Google provides a product

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.310
called Cloud AI platform that
has two features, training

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.080
and prediction.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.640
Those are managed services for
machine learning, training,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.710
and prediction.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.520
For example, if you are using
machine learning prediction

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.730
of Cloud AI platform,
then you don't

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.770
have to spend your time for
building a scalable prediction

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.080
infrastructure by yourself.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.660
Instead, the managed
service can take

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:37.690
care of the adding
more instances

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.780
or removing the instances based
on the current workload you

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.540
are getting for the
prediction system.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.020
And also, it can provide
a distributed GPU and TPU

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.590
training service.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:53.240
So you can easily get the
distributed the GPU environment

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:57.500
without spending much time
on building a GPU cluster.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.790
And also, the service can
provide the logging features

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.220
using the BigQuery
data warehouse.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.980
So that makes much
easier for building

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.270
a continuous monitoring setup.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.850
So by using Cloud
AI platform, you

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.890
can easily get those
benefits required

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.380
for the scalable
machine learning system.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.100
That includes the scheduling
of the old instances,

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.330
lifecycle management of those
continuous instances, scaling

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.600
out and scaling in
functionalities, load balancing

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.630
and dispatching the workloads,
logging, monitoring,

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.150
troubleshooting, and security.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.360
You don't have to build those
features from the scratch

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:38.990
by yourself.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.210
Instead, those
features are already

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.830
available in AI platform.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.850
But for the large
enterprises, that

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:50.310
is not the end of the story.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.210
So for example, let's imagine
a scenario where you have just

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.710
finished building
a machine learning

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:03.580
pipeline for a single
service-like recommendation.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.980
If it's a large
enterprise, then you

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:09.830
have to move onto another
project like ads optimization.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:13.150
Then you have to build another
service, fraud detection,

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.630
demand forecasting, customer
support, research, and so on.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:21.310
So without thinking about
sharing any intermediate result

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.892
or assets, you'd easily end
up with having multiple silos

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:27.100
of machine learning pipeline.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.790
That is an anti-pattern
called siloed ML.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.560
So for example, for building
a recommendation system,

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.830
maybe you are getting the
user profile or user vector

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.440
from user activities
or a product vector

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.210
from full product
description, tutoring a model

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.420
for recommendation service.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:52.780
So the solution could
be building a machine

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.490
learning service mesh.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.370
You could start with building
a monolith kind of this service

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:01.300
that has everything inside
it, but gradually you

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.100
can split your
responsibility into

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:06.100
the multiple microservices.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.880
And eventually you'd have the
bunch of the microservices that

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:14.390
can be easily composed to
build a final machine learning

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:18.360
service for the
clients or consumers.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.150
For example, in case of
the recommendation systems,

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.760
rather than building on the
monolith of the recommendation

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.450
systems, but you can
split those services

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:31.380
into the three microservices.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.230
The ML service A can
predict a user vector

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:35.940
from these activities.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.520
Service B can predict
a product vector

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.470
from product description.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.900
Then you could have
another service, C,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.450
that can those user
vector and product

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.050
vector to provide our
recommendation result so

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:53.010
that you can easily reuse
those internal assets like user

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.475
vectors and product vector
for the other purposes.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:01.270
So this is your solution
called ML service mesh,

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:04.930
but the missing link here
is the API management.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.400
And why is that?

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:10.240
Because if you are working
for a large enterprises,

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.220
you have to think
about how to share

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:15.040
those assets with the proper
business model and security

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:15.970
model.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:21.280
You cannot just expose your
APIs without any of the tracking

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.150
and monitoring for that.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.630
So missing link here
is the API management

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:27.730
for the ML service mesh.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:29.850
So I direct you past
this screen to Rakesh.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:34.002
He will be discussing
more about API management.

00:07:34.002 --> 00:07:36.170
KAZ SATO: Thank you Kaz.

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.820
So let's continue with the
description where Kaz stopped.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:42.570
So we have the ML pipelines.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.290
You want to introduce them as
part of enterprise consumption.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:47.890
So how do you go about?

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.950
You might have
internal consumers,

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.660
mostly for the ML pipelines.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.280
It might be internal consumers.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.040
But you might also have
the external consumption,

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.130
the third party, where
you don't have control.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:04.560
So the idea here is how can
you take your pipelines,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.530
or it can be other
services, and make

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.710
them easily consumable
by your consumers

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:15.120
without any friction ideally
in a self-service mode?

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.550
So this is where we're
going to talk about

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.690
on how to make it happen.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.610
Most of the companies,
most of the enterprises

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.950
on this journey moving from
monoliths into microservices.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.733
Maybe some companies are
halfway through to service

00:08:29.733 --> 00:08:31.900
or [INAUDIBLE] architectures,
but still, the journey

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.590
is going on into
breaking down monoliths

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.900
into smaller services.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:40.390
Well the idea here is to
make the services fine

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.250
grained and make
the communication

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:47.200
protocols between these
services lightweight and simple.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.190
And also, many of
the enterprises

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:53.500
are making the assumption
that the trust is established

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.430
between the services without
actually enforcing policies.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:01.000
So the idea of a good
microservices architecture

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.510
is to increase
product velocity, you

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.480
want to release your features
as often as possible,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.690
manage complexity
of the code, and you

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:12.700
want to admit heterogeneity.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.740
Each developer has
their own tool search.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:17.890
Maybe they like [? scanner ?]
some teams might like Java,

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.050
some teams might like Darknet.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:21.590
You want to admit that.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.950
And then you want to
apply security policies.

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:25.340
Very, very important.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:28.270
You want to enable that
trust, so enterprise security

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:29.170
policies.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:30.880
And then at the
same time, you want

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.420
to empower your developers to
be as quick as possible and as

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:35.560
independent as possible.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:45.090
So this is how an enterprise
services can look like.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.400
You have multiple services.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.650
They are all
communicating with APIs.

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:55.170
And by default, APIs becomes
a communication contract

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:56.730
between services.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:58.740
Every system that
can receive requests

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.360
has some kind of an EPA.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.790
They can be RESTful,
they, can be SOAP,

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.300
or maybe thousand different
ways of doing APIs.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.570
Or maybe they're using
GraphQL, gipc, YAML.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:12.870
So there's a mesh of services
within the enterprise.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:17.100
And then when a consumer,
from outside or from inside

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.720
calls an API, they orchestrate
the calls within the mesh

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.740
and get back with the response.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.230
So we are getting into
some kind of a mesh here.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:32.910
Let's see how to enable this.

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:36.420
Let's dig in deep a little bit.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.030
We have service A, we
have service B. Now

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.830
these are microservices.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.960
How do you enable
communications with them?

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.500
The best way is to
have mutual dealers.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.600
Ideally, you want
to have certificates

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:50.652
for each of the
services, and once you

00:10:50.652 --> 00:10:53.110
have unique certificates, you
can enable those certificates

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.920
on the other services.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.490
And you also want to
introduce centrally described

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:03.700
communication policies
like lead tries, timeout,

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:07.020
circuit breakers if a
call is taking too long.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.080
You want to enable routing
so that if the services are

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.590
located in different
regions, different teams

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.900
to enable the routing
in efficient way.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:18.760
And on top of it,
you want to enable

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.570
[INAUDIBLE] the
authentication authorization.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.880
Maybe not every
service is consumable

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.280
by the other service,
so to be informed,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:29.030
you authorize those services.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:33.120
When this is happening, you want
to collect analytics and also

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.380
monitor the services.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:36.540
You want to know at
any point in time

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.910
whether the service
is up or down.

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.920
And at the same time,
you want to enable

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.000
some kind of telemetry so that
you know who is consuming what

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.540
at any point in time.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:49.530
So we need a sort of a
support system to enable this.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.300
Let us see how to
make this happen.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.620
So let's delegate
all this complexity

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:03.470
of this communication protocols
to a standalone, something

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.090
called Envoy.

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.690
Envoy is a high
performance plug-able proxy

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:13.370
providing inflow networking
and observability for increased

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:15.240
service communication.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:20.570
So Envoy was built on
learning from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:23.960
Envoy is very popular
and widely used.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:27.050
And it's very widely
used inside Google also.

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:30.410
Envoy uses what we
called a sidecar pattern.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.620
And the sidecar
pattern is better

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:36.110
than embedding
different [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.450
and making it a big monolith.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.340
So with this
pattern, it's easier

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.730
to support services implemented
in multiple languages.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:47.270
And also, you can upgrade
the proxies independent

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:48.240
of other services.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:54.740
So this is where a good
control plane will help

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.660
you to manage your services.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:04.220
So me as a data scientist,
at this point I'm thinking

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.600
is this challenging
or is this simple?

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:11.100
Now a model has become part
of a mesh architecture.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.260
It's no more a silo.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:19.230
So models are part of a big
piece in the enterprise.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.960
So let's start digging deep now.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:25.500
We want to introduce the control
plane, which is a service mesh.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.480
What does a service
mesh help us to do?

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:32.190
It'll help us to connect
between the services, the flow

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.140
of traffic between
services, it'll

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:36.690
help to secure the endpoint
between the services

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:40.290
and encrypted communication,
very, very important.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:45.210
And you want to control this
by applying security policies.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:46.920
And then you want to
observe and collect

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.500
metrics for monitoring
and analytics purpose.

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:54.710
Let's dig in a little bit deep.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:58.210
This is where Istio
comes into play.

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:02.350
Istio starts with the Envoy
proxy as a sidecar pattern,

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:06.320
and then access our top policies
to help and manage [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.330
the distributed set of proxies.

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:12.210
As you can see in this picture
here, you have the Envoy,

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:14.240
you have the control
plane, and then

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:19.530
we'll also introduce what is
called as API management here.

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:20.630
So let us talk to this.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:24.700
So how an API management
layer will help you.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.090
You have the services.

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:29.810
And you want to enable them,
both for internal consumption

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:31.730
and your external consumption.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:34.070
And let's start with
an example, and I'll

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:36.515
explain to you on how
the API management

00:14:36.515 --> 00:14:38.080
layer will help you to do this.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:44.360
So let's take this example
where we have a legacy app,

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:49.980
and you have a monolith app,
and you have your consumption

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:50.960
layer.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:53.690
All these modeled apps,
you have the legacy apps,

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:58.490
and then most of the
enterprises have an API gateway.

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:02.630
In this case, Apigee API
gateway is a GCP offering

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:07.220
to provide you a full-fledged
size offering to manage APIs.

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:09.955
Most of the enterprises
enable their services

00:15:09.955 --> 00:15:11.930
through the API layer.

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:13.940
API layer kind of
acts as a switch

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:17.660
to switch between different
services in the backend.

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:23.400
And then you have
your internal apps.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.630
Most places, internal
apps are directly

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:28.080
going against the services.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.032
There's not much
of API management

00:15:30.032 --> 00:15:30.990
going on in that layer.

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:37.850
Now let us go through this
journey of breaking down

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:40.640
a monolith into microservices.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:43.340
So let's take the Monolith
2 and break it down

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.170
into smaller services.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.510
At the same time, you're
updating the internal app

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:51.440
to consume your microservices
as well as the monolith.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.650
And at the same time,
you're updating your gateway

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:57.880
to also use their services,
the new microservices.

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:04.360
And in the process,
what you're doing

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:07.000
is you're introducing
new capabilities

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.880
by adding more microservices,
and also you're

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:15.290
introducing ML pipelines
using Cloud ML platform.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.900
This is where you're adding
more capabilities where

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:19.960
your internal apps
are being updated

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:22.390
to consume these
new capabilities,

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:23.890
and at the same
time, you're keeping

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:27.460
it transparent for your
external consumption

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:31.120
to use in your capabilities.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:34.450
So this is, as you
can see in this,

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:38.540
the onset of proliferation
of different services.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:40.210
So this is a problem.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:41.910
Probably a good problem to have.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.020
Let us see how we
can simplify this.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.350
So this is where
Istio control plane

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:56.491
will help you to build a
service mesh architecture.

00:16:56.491 --> 00:17:00.340
In this diagram, you'll
see the service mesh

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:03.310
is kind of helping to
manage the communication

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:05.500
between the services.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:11.819
You also see the legacy systems
using the legacy service

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:17.050
as being exposed to the gateway
to the third-party system.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:19.150
And also the internal
apps are using the legacy,

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:20.800
but at the same time,
the internal apps

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:26.619
are now being consuming
the services from the mesh.

00:17:26.619 --> 00:17:30.490
And in fact, the
gateway is acting

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:33.730
like a nice little
switch to switch

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:38.200
between the legacy and the new
layer of mesh architecture.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:42.700
You can also see what is
called as Apigee adapter,

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:46.330
and this is where you
get the API policies.

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:48.600
Now what does the
adapter help you to do?

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:52.990
It'll help you to centrally
govern or manage APIs.

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:56.260
It enables API
publishing, visibility

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.410
of the APIs by publishing
them into portals,

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.510
enables governance,
good governance,

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:05.710
by applying your best practices,
and it collects the analytics.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.410
Specifically from a
security perspective,

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:12.040
the adapter will help you
enforce in the Envoy things

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:16.390
like verifying the OAuth
token on the API keys,

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.790
checking if there's
enough authorization

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:22.615
to consume the services, and
other things like a quota.

00:18:22.615 --> 00:18:25.060
If you have some kind of
a quota on the service,

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:26.290
it'll enforce that.

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:29.320
If you have rate limiting,
it'll enforce that.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.110
If you have text
protection to validate

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:35.230
any text coming on the
APIs, it'll enforce that.

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:38.710
So basically
adapter's helping you

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:41.830
to do these kind of
security policies.

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:44.020
And Istio service
mesh is helping

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:48.740
you to enable the communication
between the services.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.190
This is kind of a
clean model and where

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:54.040
the suppression of concerns
is very clearly maintained.

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:02.240
So let us understand
how this is happening.

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:05.790
So Envoy supports a
long list of filters,

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:09.560
and these filters are written
in C++ and compiled into Envoy

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:10.880
itself.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:13.430
Apigee adapter uses
one specific filter

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:18.020
called external authorization
external outfitter, which

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:21.770
is designed to allow Envoy
to delegate authorization

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:24.080
decisions to an external system.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:29.250
This is where Apigee is
registered and gets a control.

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:31.790
So let's see how the
traffic flow happens here.

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:35.480
So when API call
comes into Envoy,

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:39.320
the Envoy gives the
control to Apigee adapter.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:43.320
In this case, we call it
as Apigee's remote service.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.290
Apigee's remote
service will go back

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:47.780
with either a yay or a nay.

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:51.770
So what it will do is
it'll enforce the policies

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:54.680
that the API
publisher has designed

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:56.170
for this particular consumption.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:20:00.030
So it can be OAuth, it can
be quotas-type production,

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:00.890
rate limiting.

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:04.310
Any policies they have,
it's enforced here.

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:06.680
If that app responds
back with a yay,

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:10.390
then it'll go back to
your backend service,

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:13.730
and then the call as
passes in your backend.

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:18.170
If it is a nay, then an error
is sent back to your consumer.

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:20.870
So Apigee's remote service is
acting as a policy decision

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:22.450
point.

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:26.790
And it is to Envoy to unload
the API consumption [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:29.510
Now how does this work in a
very high-performance system?

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:33.920
How can you get those
hundreds and thousands of TPLs

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:35.530
in API call?

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:38.570
So first the communication
between the proxy

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:41.970
and the Apigee's
remote services gRPC.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:45.230
So this is very good for
speed and efficiency.

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:48.320
And then, the Apigee's remote
service talks to its management

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.510
plane and downloads
anything that

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:52.910
is required to make
their decision, which

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:56.967
includes the policies,
keys, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:20:56.967 --> 00:20:57.925
anything that required.

00:20:57.925 --> 00:21:00.690
It's all cached inside
the remote servers.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:04.620
So this is how the
Apigee's remote service

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:09.640
is built for a high-performing
support system.

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:12.670
So let's try to bring
everything together.

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:15.090
So why use API management
in your service mesh?

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:24.415
So you don't want any of the
services to be in a siloed.

00:21:24.415 --> 00:21:28.810
Whether it is your Cloud
AI prediction models,

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:30.940
whether it can be
a microservice,

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:32.330
it can be anything.

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:33.640
It needs to be discovered.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:35.410
And how do you discover them?

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:38.020
So with Apigee, you can
have a portal where you

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:40.820
can publish all your services.

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:43.180
And this portal can
be designed in a way

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:45.940
that the consumers can
act in a self-service way.

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:49.840
There is no more
whitelisting the firewalls,

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.330
whitelisting your APIs.

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:55.420
Someone can come and say I
need access to certain APIs,

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:58.570
and then your API producer
can get a request,

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:01.720
and then they can
upload the request.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.720
And then you can
modernize your services.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:09.890
All the stuff on the legacy,
your old monolith applications,

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:12.580
everything can be hidden
by a nice little APIs using

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.680
REST or any standards
that you use

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:17.900
and expose them in a good way.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:22.090
And then no more exchanging
those PDF files or Word files.

00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:25.750
You can have nice documentation
wherein you view the required

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:29.290
technical aspects for
the API, and the consumer

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:32.860
can comment and try those
APIs before you want

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:35.900
to use it in the application.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:39.210
And then you can have
reports on the services.

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:40.490
This is very, very critical.

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:43.070
At any point, you should be
able to answer the questions

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:46.300
like who are my top
five consumers, or what

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:49.040
are my SLAs that
were satisfied today,

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:53.280
or what was my performance,
what kind of products

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:56.070
are used from a business aspect?

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:59.000
So you should be able to answer
these kind of questions using

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:01.090
the analytics that you covered.

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:02.840
And in a large
enterprise, governance

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:04.800
becomes very, very important.

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:07.680
You want some kind of
best practices enforced

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.110
across the teams,
and this is critical.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:17.080
You can enforce things like
CI/CD, linting, code standards,

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:19.470
all these kinds of things
across the enterprise.

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:22.810
You can enable and enforce them.

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:25.340
Now once you have the APIs,
it's very, very critical

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:26.570
you monitor them.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:29.030
At any point, you should
know which service is up,

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:32.420
which service is slowing down
before your consumers tell you,

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:33.960
which is very, very important.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:37.070
And then once you have this
nice little system working,

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:38.990
you want to start
making money off of it.

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:41.090
You want to accept a
credit card and start

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:42.870
charging your consumers.

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.278
And this is where the
monetization module in Apigee

00:23:45.278 --> 00:23:46.070
comes into picture.

00:23:46.070 --> 00:23:49.160
You can plug it in and
define your policies

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.650
on how to monetize the APIs.

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:54.050
Maybe not all APIs
need to be monetized.

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:57.830
Maybe you pick a few business
APIs which are critical.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.870
You only monetize a
certain set of APIs.

00:23:59.870 --> 00:24:04.010
These kind of behaviors
can be [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:05.651
and with a good API
management layer.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.540
So we have a solution page
which ties up all of this.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:16.120
And you should be able to
try it, make a prediction

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:20.800
model in the Cloud AI platform,
and then expose that AI

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:23.860
model using the Apigee
engine, and then

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:25.820
send it to your consumers.

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:27.520
So all this in a
frictionless way.

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:30.920
We have a solution page
explaining all of this.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:32.620
I would say the biggest
takeaway for you

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:35.320
in this session will
be once you have this

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:38.530
modeled, how can your
model the other services,

00:24:38.530 --> 00:24:42.040
how can you make your
consumption of these services

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:43.990
seamless, frictionless.

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:47.530
You want the consumption
to be the easiest of all

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:52.450
so that your services are
actually consumed a lot faster,

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:54.700
giving the benefit
to the enterprise.

00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:56.840
The impact is a lot
more faster if you

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:58.630
remove those kinds of friction.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:00.340
So the biggest
takeaway will be how

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:05.290
do you bring in your services
in a way that can be consumed

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:08.650
without any friction?

